      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Expeditions to the New World</strong><br>\nRaleigh organized and sponsored expeditions to America, although he never actually visited the continent himself. His most famous venture was the attempt to colonize Roanoke Island, which would later be known as \"The Lost Colony\" due to the mysterious disappearance of its settlers. Raleigh's explorations paved the way for future English colonization.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conflict with Spain</strong><br>\nAs a strong proponent of English expansion, Raleigh inevitably clashed with Spain, the dominant colonial power of the time. He was involved in the capture of a Spanish treasure fleet and the sacking of Spanish settlements. These acts made him a hero in England but a notorious figure to the Spanish, contributing to the mounting tensions between the two empires.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Contributions</strong><br>\nIn addition to his adventures, Raleigh was also a noted writer. He penned \"The Discovery of Guiana\" and \"The History of the World,\" works that combined his experiences with his visions for England's role in global affairs. His writing style and insights into Elizabethan politics and exploration are still studied today.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
