          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Downfall and Imprisonment</strong><br>\nRaleigh's fortune reversed dramatically after Elizabeth's death in 1603. He was accused of plotting against her successor, King James I, and was imprisoned in the Tower of London. Although initially sentenced to death, his sentence was commuted, and he spent many years in confinement, during which he continued to write.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Voyage and Execution</strong><br>\nIn a bid to restore his position, Raleigh was released to lead an expedition to find the legendary city of El Dorado. However, the expedition was a failure, and upon his return, he was arrested again. The previous charges of treason were brought forth, and Raleigh was executed in 1618. His execution marked the end of an era of Elizabethan exploration and expansion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Sir Walter Raleigh</strong><br>\nSir Walter Raleigh left a complex legacy. He is remembered as a visionary who promoted English exploration and colonization, a patron of the arts, and a writer of great importance. His life reflected the ambitions and conflicts of Elizabethan England and the fragile nature of favoritism and political fortune in the royal court.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
